---
import DocPage from '../../components/DocPage.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
---
<DocPage eyebrow="DOCS / CI" title="GitHub Actions" lead="gua-project/gua-tester provides versioned CI workflows for Gua UI tests in Godot 4.7 and Unity 6 projects." description="Godot and Unity inputs, credentials, artifacts, and visual reports provided by Gua Tester Actions." section="docs">
  <Callout title="Gua Tester Actions v3.2"><p>Use the <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua-tester/releases/tag/v3.2">published v3.2 actions</a>. The action version (<code>@v3.2</code>), Gua version (for example <code>gua-v1.1.0</code>), and Godot/Unity version are independent selections.</p></Callout>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="Godot and Unity files downloaded by CI"><p>The Godot action downloads <code>gua-godot-addon-*.zip</code>; the Unity workflow downloads <code>com.link1345.gua-*.tgz</code> from the selected Gua release. The <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/releases/tag/gua-v1.1.0">1.1.0 release</a> currently contains neither. Selecting 1.1.0 therefore fails during file download before tests start.</p><p>This is a missing distribution file, not a gua-tester version ceiling. The <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua-tester/blob/v3.2/godot/action.yml">Godot action</a> and <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua-tester/blob/v3.2/.github/workflows/unity.yml">Unity workflow</a> validate release files and their contents without imposing a maximum Gua version. With no tag selected, they choose the latest stable release containing the required file, which may be older than the latest Gua.</p><p>The download examples below select the file-bearing <code>gua-v1.0.7</code>; pin their test project's Gua NuGet packages to <code>1.0.7</code> too. For 1.1.0, prepare the game integration using the <a href="/godot/ui/">Godot</a> or <a href="/unity/ui/">Unity</a> installation guide. For Godot, use <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua-tester/blob/v3.2/setup-godot/action.yml">setup-godot</a> to install only the engine and run <code>dotnet test</code> against your prepared project. For Unity, build and test a project containing the prepared package. The automatic-download workflow needs the 1.1.0 release files before it can be used for that version.</p></Callout>
  <h2>Godot 4.7</h2>
  <p>The Godot composite action downloads the official Godot build matching its runner, links the released Gua addon, exports <code>GODOT_EXECUTABLE</code>, prepares project imports, and runs the external <code>Gua.Testing.Godot</code> test project.</p>
  <CodeBlock title=".github/workflows/godot.yml" lang="yaml" code={`jobs:
  godot:
    runs-on: ubuntu-22.04
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: gua-project/gua-tester/godot@v3.2
        with:
          project-path: game
          test-project: tests/GuaTester.Tests.csproj
          godot-version: "4.7"
          godot-status: stable
          gua-plugin-tag: gua-v1.0.7`} />
  <div class="grid-2"><div class="mini-card"><strong>test-project (required)</strong><p>The <code>.csproj</code> that references <code>Gua.Testing.Godot</code>.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>project-path</strong><p>Godot project path; default <code>game</code>.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>godot-version / godot-status</strong><p>Default <code>4.7</code> and <code>stable</code>.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>test-logger</strong><p>Default <code>trx;LogFileName=godot.trx</code>.</p></div></div>
  <p>The outputs are <code>godot-executable</code> and <code>addon-path</code>. Component actions remain available as <code>gua-project/gua-tester/setup-godot@v3.2</code> and <code>gua-project/gua-tester/link-gua-gdscript-addon@v3.2</code>.</p>

  <h2 id="prepared-godot">Test a prepared Godot project at 1.1.0</h2><p>This example requires the 1.1.0 add-on for the runner to be present in <code>game/addons/gua</code> and the test project to use Gua 1.1.0 NuGet packages. <code>setup-godot</code> installs only Godot and does not download Gua release files.</p>
  <CodeBlock title="Test without downloading Gua" lang="yaml" code={"jobs:\n  prepared-godot:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-22.04\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-dotnet@v4\n        with:\n          dotnet-version: \"10.0.x\"\n      - uses: gua-project/gua-tester/setup-godot@v3.2\n        with:\n          godot-version: \"4.7\"\n      - name: Import and test the prepared game\n        shell: pwsh\n        run: |\n          & \"$env:GODOT_EXECUTABLE\" --headless --editor --path game --import\n          if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { exit $LASTEXITCODE }\n          dotnet test tests/GuaTester.Tests.csproj --configuration Release"} />

  <h2>Unity 6 desktop Mono</h2>
  <p>The Unity reusable workflow installs a released Gua UPM package, builds the requested Mono Player with GameCI, transfers it to the matching runner, and runs external NUnit tests with <code>GUA_UNITY_PLAYER</code>.</p>
  <CodeBlock title=".github/workflows/unity.yml" lang="yaml" code={`jobs:
  unity:
    if: github.event_name != 'pull_request' || github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository
    uses: gua-project/gua-tester/.github/workflows/unity.yml@v3.2
    with:
      project-path: game
      scene-path: Assets/Scenes/Title.unity
      test-project: tests/GuaTester.Unity.Tests.csproj
      artifact-key: game
      platform: LinuxX64
      unity-version: auto
      gua-tag: gua-v1.0.7
    secrets:
      UNITY_EMAIL: \${{ secrets.UNITY_EMAIL }}
      UNITY_PASSWORD: \${{ secrets.UNITY_PASSWORD }}
      UNITY_LICENSE: \${{ secrets.UNITY_LICENSE }}
      UNITY_SERIAL: \${{ secrets.UNITY_SERIAL }}`} />
  <p>Required inputs are <code>project-path</code>, <code>scene-path</code>, <code>test-project</code>, and a caller-unique <code>artifact-key</code>. Optional inputs include <code>unity-version</code>, <code>gua-repository</code>, <code>gua-tag</code>, <code>dotnet-version</code>, <code>configuration</code>, <code>test-logger</code>, and <code>artifact-path</code>. <code>checkout-repository</code> and <code>checkout-ref</code> support fixtures in another repository. Use a matrix value for <code>artifact-key</code> when invoking the workflow more than once in a run.</p>
  <div class="grid-2"><div class="mini-card"><strong>Personal license</strong><p>Pass <code>UNITY_EMAIL</code>, <code>UNITY_PASSWORD</code>, and <code>UNITY_LICENSE</code>.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>Professional license</strong><p>Pass <code>UNITY_EMAIL</code>, <code>UNITY_PASSWORD</code>, and <code>UNITY_SERIAL</code>.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>Supported target</strong><p>Unity 6000.5+, Mono, and the four desktop RIDs.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>Artifacts</strong><p>Unity logs, Player files, TRX, diagnostics, and visual results.</p></div></div>
  <Callout kind="warn" title="Protect Unity credentials"><p>GitHub does not pass Actions secrets to fork pull requests. Skip this job for untrusted forks and do not use <code>pull_request_target</code> to execute untrusted game code with Unity credentials.</p></Callout>
  <Callout kind="tip" title="Keep Gua versions aligned"><p>Pin <code>gua-tag</code> and use the matching <code>Gua.Testing.Unity</code> NuGet version. The selected release must contain the add-on or UPM files required by the runner. The workflow does not support IL2CPP, Editor Play Mode CI, IMGUI, or EditorWindow automation.</p></Callout>

  <h2 id="visual-report">Visual difference reports</h2>
  <p><code>gua-project/gua-tester/visual-report@v3.2</code> converts <code>Gua.Testing.Visual</code> manifests and PNGs into a static report for a workflow artifact or GitHub Pages. It is engine-neutral.</p>
  <CodeBlock title="Upload a failed visual report" lang="yaml" code={`- name: Upload visual report
  if: failure()
  uses: gua-project/gua-tester/visual-report@v3.2
  with:
    artifact-path: artifacts/gua
    test-outcome: failure
    upload-target: workflow`} />
  <p>Set <code>upload-target</code> to <code>pages</code> only in a trusted main or manually dispatched run, then deploy the generated Pages artifact from a separate job with <code>pages: write</code> and <code>id-token: write</code>.</p>
  <Callout kind="warn" title="Screenshot visibility"><p>Screenshots may contain secrets or personal information rendered by the game. Review captured content before publishing reports.</p></Callout>

  <h2>Pin the dependencies</h2><p>Use <code>gua-project/gua-tester/...@v3.2</code> for actions/workflows and align the Gua tag with the test project's NuGet version. Choose test APIs and optional packages in <a href="/godot/testing/">Godot testing</a> or <a href="/unity/testing/">Unity testing</a>.</p>
</DocPage>
